# Third-party licences — Baxters Stereo Mix

Baxters Stereo Mix is **MIT** (see `LICENSE`). It links third-party code whose
licences impose obligations of their own. They are recorded here in full,
because a licence obligation that lives only in someone's memory is one release
away from being forgotten.

---

## LAME (libmp3lame) — LGPL, **statically linked**

MP3 encoding is provided by **LAME 3.100**, reached through two Rust crates:

| component | version | licence |
|---|---|---|
| `mp3lame-encoder` | 0.2.2 | LGPL-3.0 (crate metadata) |
| `mp3lame-sys` | 0.1.11 | LGPL-3.0 (crate metadata) |
| LAME itself, vendored inside `mp3lame-sys` | **3.100** | GNU Library GPL **v2 or, at your option, any later version** |

### The linkage is STATIC, and that is the fact that decides everything else

`mp3lame-sys` does not link a system `libmp3lame.so`. It **vendors LAME's C
source** (49 `.c` files under `lame-3.100/`) and compiles it into the binary:

    build.rs:48    .enable_static()
    build.rs:58    println!("cargo:rustc-link-lib=static=mp3lame");
    build.rs:138   cc.compile("mp3lame")

So every Baxters Stereo Mix binary **contains** LAME. Static linking is the case
the LGPL is most concerned with, because a user cannot simply drop in their own
build of the library.

LAME's per-file headers say "either version 2 of the License, or (at your
option) any later version", so LGPL-2 code and the LGPL-3.0 wrappers combine
without conflict; the combination is used under **LGPL-3.0**.

### How the obligations are met

**Relinking (LGPL-3 §4(d)) — satisfied by construction.** LGPL-3 §4(d) requires
either a shared-library mechanism, or that the user be given what they need to
relink the application against a modified library. Baxters Stereo Mix ships its
**complete source under MIT**, so anyone can substitute a modified LAME and
rebuild. Nothing further is required for this clause.

*If Stereo Mix is ever shipped as a binary WITHOUT its source, this stops being
true* and one of two things must happen: switch to dynamic linking against the
system `libmp3lame0`, or ship object files sufficient for relinking. Ubuntu
packages `libmp3lame0`, so the dynamic route is available.

**Notice (LGPL-3 §4(a), §4(c)) — this document, plus `licenses/`.**
The licence texts travel with the work:

    licenses/LGPL-3.0.txt     GNU Lesser General Public License v3
    licenses/GPL-3.0.txt      GNU General Public License v3  (LGPL-3 §4(b): LGPL-3
                              is an addendum to GPL-3, so both must be included)
    licenses/LAME-COPYING.txt LAME's own COPYING (GNU Library GPL v2)
    licenses/LAME-LICENSE.txt LAME's own LICENSE note

**Modifications — none.** LAME is used exactly as vendored by `mp3lame-sys`
0.1.11. Nothing in this repository patches LAME. If that ever changes, LAME's
LICENSE requires those modifications be released.

**Acknowledgement.** LAME's own LICENSE asks that projects using it say so and
link to the project. Baxters Stereo Mix uses the LAME MP3 encoder —
<https://lame.sourceforge.io/>. Not strictly an LGPL requirement, but asked for
by the authors and freely given.

LAME is copyright its contributors; see `licenses/LAME-COPYING.txt` and the
crate source for the full notices.

